---
title: Troubleshooting
description: The failures that actually happen, and what causes them.
section: Reference
---

Collected gotchas. Most of these are silent — they do not raise an error in the dashboard, which is
exactly why they are worth writing down.

## `redirect_uri_mismatch` on every login

By far the most common integration failure.

Redirect URIs are matched **exactly**. Not by prefix, not by pattern, and not ignoring the trailing
slash:

```text
registered: https://app.example.com/callback
sent:       https://app.example.com/callback/     ✗ different URI
sent:       http://app.example.com/callback       ✗ different scheme
sent:       https://app.example.com/callback?x=1  ✗ different URI
```

Copy the URI your application actually sends — from the browser's network tab, not from memory —
and register that string.

## Users never receive password-reset or verification email

There is no working [email provider](/docs/email), or its credentials are wrong.

A bad SMTP password does not surface when you save the provider. It surfaces as users who never
receive anything and have no way to tell you. Use the **Send test email** button; it is there
specifically to turn a silent failure into a loud one.

## The risk engine never flags a foreign login

`GEOIP_CITY_DB_PATH` is not set on the server.

Without the MaxMind databases, `NEW_COUNTRY` and `IMPOSSIBLE_TRAVEL` **can never fire** — two of the
eight [signals](/docs/risk-engine), including the best one for catching a stolen password being used
from another continent. Everything else keeps working, and nothing warns you.

## Enabling `client_credentials` has no effect

The OAuth client form offers a `client_credentials` checkbox, but the token endpoint does not
implement that grant and OIDC discovery does not advertise it. There is currently **no
machine-to-machine flow**. Ticking the box changes nothing; do not design against it.

## A user was logged out unexpectedly

Look for `token.reuse_detected` in the [audit log](/docs/audit-log).

Refresh tokens rotate. If one is presented twice, GateKeeper concludes it leaked and revokes the
entire token family, which logs the user out. The usual innocent cause is an application that
persists a refresh token and uses it from two processes at once, or one that retries a failed
token request with the same token. The non-innocent cause is that a refresh token really was
stolen — which is exactly what the mechanism is for.

## A role change did not take effect immediately

Roles are re-resolved on every token issue **and every refresh**, but an access token already in
flight keeps the claims it was minted with until it expires — up to 15 minutes.

If you need instant revocation, deactivate the user (which stops the refresh) rather than only
removing a role.

## I lost the client secret

It cannot be recovered. It is stored as an Argon2 hash, so GateKeeper genuinely does not have it.

Rotate it on the **Credentials** tab, and deploy the new one — the old secret stops working the
moment the new one is issued.

## Admin API calls succeed when they should be rejected

`REQUIRE_ADMIN_AUTH` is `false`.

That downgrades the admin guard to best-effort: requests are attributed in the audit log but are no
longer *blocked*. It exists for local development and must be `true` anywhere real.
